= The Library

Herein is information about the party, the state it works in, and the rules this game follows.

@menu

- @timeline: Timeline, 1918 to 1923
- @later: What happened in our history, 1924 to 1933
- @factions: The factions of the KPD
- @moscow: Moscow and the Comintern
- @paramilitaries: Armed groups
- @rules: How the game decides things
- @backSpecialScene: Exit library.

@timeline
go-to: menu

**1918.** On 9 November the Kaiser falls and the republic is proclaimed. The majority Social Democrats under Friedrich Ebert make a pact with the army command to keep order. In December the Spartacus League and other radicals found the KPD.

**1919.** In January the Berlin uprising is put down by the army and the Freikorps. Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht are murdered on 15 January. The Weimar National Assembly meets in February, and the Treaty of Versailles is signed in June.

**1920.** The Kapp putsch in March fails before a general strike. In the June elections the republican parties lose their majority. In December the left of the USPD joins the KPD and makes it a mass party.

**1921.** The March Action, an ill-prepared rising in central Germany, fails and costs the party thousands of members. Paul Levi, who criticises it publicly, is expelled.

**1922.** The ministers Matthias Erzberger (1921) and Walther Rathenau (June 1922) are assassinated by the nationalist Right. The mark begins to fall quickly.

**1923.** French and Belgian troops occupy the Ruhr in January. The inflation reaches its end in the autumn, and the year ends with the Rentenmark, the Munich putsch and the ban on the KPD. This is where the game begins.

@later
go-to: menu

This is the history the game departs from. Your party may do something else.

**1924.** Rentenmark stabilisation; the KPD wins 12.6 per cent in May and falls to 8.9 in December. The Dawes Plan brings American loans. The RFB is founded in July.

**1925.** Ebert dies; Hindenburg is elected president in the second round after the KPD runs Thälmann against Marx. The Comintern's fifth congress and the "Bolshevisation" of the party have already begun.

**1926.** The referendum on the expropriation of the princes wins 14.5 million votes and fails. Fischer and Maslow are expelled from the KPD.

**1928.** The SPD wins the May election. The Comintern's Sixth Congress proclaims the Third Period. The Wittorf affair ends with Thälmann restored by Stalin's intervention.

**1929.** Blutmai in Berlin; the RFB is banned. The New York crash in October. The Young Plan referendum fails in December, but gives Hitler a national platform.

**1930.** Brüning governs by emergency decree; in September the NSDAP wins 18.3 per cent.

**1931.** Bank crisis in July; the KPD joins the Nazi-backed Prussian referendum on Moscow's orders. The Harzburg Front in October.

**1932.** Hindenburg is re-elected; Papen lifts the SA ban; Altona; the Prussian coup on 20 July. The NSDAP wins 37.3 per cent in July and 33.1 in November.

**1933.** Hitler is appointed on 30 January. The Reichstag burns on 27 February and the KPD is crushed. The Enabling Act passes on 23 March.

@factions
go-to: menu

The party is not one body. Four currents fight over the line.

**The Right** (Brandler, Thalheimer): the united front with Social Democratic workers, workers' governments in the states, patient work in the unions and factory councils. Its danger is waiting on leaders who will not fight.

**The Left** (Fischer, Maslow, later Thälmann's followers): an offensive, armed workers' units, a hard break with the SPD leaders. Its danger is calling a fight that the workers have not chosen.

**The Centre** (Meyer, Zetkin, Pieck): mass politics, unity of the party, mediation between the two. Its danger is that it has no line of its own.

**The Moscow apparatus**: the faction of those whose first loyalty is the line from Moscow. Its danger is that Moscow is far away and has its own interests.

Factions that did not yet exist in 1923 appear later. The Conciliators (Ewert, Eisler) form in 1927. The Communist Opposition (KPO) and the Leninbund do not take part in the game's contests; they are outside the party after their expulsions.

@moscow
go-to: menu

The Communist International, the Comintern, is run from Moscow, and its leaders are fighting for power in the Russian party. When Lenin is incapacitated, the troika of Zinoviev, Kamenev and Stalin faces Trotsky and the Left Opposition. Which group leads in Moscow decides which line the International expects from us.

In this game Moscow's balance of power is a number you can see on the status page, and it moves with your delegations. Trust measures how far Moscow believes the party. A line that Moscow does not favour wears its patience down in five steps, from a warning letter to removal by decree.

@paramilitaries
go-to: menu

**The Reichswehr** is the professional army, limited to 100,000 men by the Versailles treaty. It acts against the Left more readily than against the Right.

**The Freikorps and the Bavarian leagues** are volunteer units of former soldiers, tolerated or encouraged by the state in Bavaria.

**The Proletarian Hundreds** are the workers' defence units the KPD built in 1923, strongest in Saxony and Thuringia and in Hamburg. From July 1924 the Red Front Fighters' League (the RFB) takes their place.

**The SA** is the NSDAP's paramilitary wing, which grows after the Munich putsch.

@rules
go-to: menu

Nothing in this game depends on dice. If a result looks surprising, the reason is a number on the status page or in the event text.

The party line is held by the strongest faction, and a change of line needs a contest at a conference. The contest is decided by strength, Moscow's favour, dissent and your own backing.

The anti-fascist front (status page, 0 to 100) adds up joint work with the SPD, defence units, Anti-Fascist Action and its actions. The Prussian strike of July 1932 and the general strike of 30 January 1933 succeed at fixed thresholds that the event text names.

Under the Third Period, every point of joint work with the SPD costs Moscow's patience: the more you build the front, the more you risk the line from Moscow.

The decision at Chemnitz in October 1923 is decided by readiness, the sum of unity with the SPD left, Hundreds, strikes, workers' governments and Moscow's backing.

Governments depend on which faction leads the party, Moscow's trust and joint work with the SPD.

The original game was written by Autumn Chen. This mod keeps its engine and look.
